<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
        <!--
          GS-android-systembars: PAINT THE SYSTEM BARS.

          Capacitor's stock theme sets neither statusBarColor nor navigationBarColor, so both fall
          back to the platform default — an OPAQUE LIGHT bar. Against this game's dark UI that reads
          as a white strip pinned to the bottom of the screen, which play-testing reported as a
          leftover "browser bar". It is the Android navigation bar; there is no browser in the shell.

          Why it only showed on ONE device: with targetSdk 36, Android 15+ ENFORCES edge-to-edge, so
          on a Pixel 9a the bars go transparent and the app draws behind them (which is what
          GS-play-safearea exists to handle). An older Android — the Galaxy S20 FE tops out well
          before 15 — does no such thing and simply honours the theme, defaults included. Same APK,
          two completely different results, and only the older one looked broken.

          Matching them to the app background makes the bars disappear into the game on the older
          devices, and changes nothing on the edge-to-edge ones (transparent wins there). The light*
          flags force LIGHT icons, since dark-on-dark would be unreadable; both are ignored on the
          API levels that predate them, which is why they are safe to declare with minSdk 24.
        -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/gsSystemBar</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/gsSystemBar</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
    </style>


    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/splash</item>
        <!-- Same treatment on the launch screen, so the bars don't flash light before the game paints. -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/gsSystemBar</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/gsSystemBar</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>